You can also use a for loop to do something to each item in a vector.

For example, in the below, we loop through the animals in our vector `wildlife`. Inside the loop, the value of the variable `animal` changes on each iteration.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
wildlife <- c("fox", "otter", "badger", "squirrel", "hedgehog")

for (animal in wildlife) {
  print(animal)
}
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] "fox"
[1] "otter"
[1] "badger"
[1] "squirrel"
[1] "hedgehog"
```


:::
:::


And, again, you can add as many steps as you like to perform during each iteration.

Here, we check if each animal name ends with the letters `er`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
wildlife <- c("fox", "otter", "badger", "squirrel", "hedgehog")

for (animal in wildlife) {
  has_er <- endsWith(animal, "er")
  print(paste(animal, "ends with er:", has_er))
}
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] "fox ends with er: FALSE"
[1] "otter ends with er: TRUE"
[1] "badger ends with er: TRUE"
[1] "squirrel ends with er: FALSE"
[1] "hedgehog ends with er: FALSE"
```


