## Storing results from loops {#sec-storing_results}

We sometimes want to store the results of running our loop in a vector or data frame.

For example, here we store all of the cubed numbers from our example above in a vector called `cubed_nums`. Before we start the loop, we create an empty vector using `c()`. Then, each time we pass through the loop, we use `c()` to append the new result to the end of the vector. Once the loop has finished, `cubed_nums` contains the results from every iteration.



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
cubed_nums <- c()

for (i in seq_len(20)) {
  res <- i^3
  cubed_nums <- c(cubed_nums, res)
}

print(cubed_nums)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
 [1]    1    8   27   64  125  216  343  512  729 1000 1331 1728 2197 2744 3375
[16] 4096 4913 5832 6859 8000
```


:::
:::


Similarly, we can store the results of our loop in a data frame. On each iteration through the loop, we add a new row with our newly processed data.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
animal_df <- tibble()

wildlife <- c("fox", "otter", "badger", "squirrel", "hedgehog")

for (animal in wildlife) {
  has_er <- endsWith(animal, "er")
  current_df <- tibble(
    Animal_Name = animal,
    Ends_in_ER = has_er
  )
  animal_df <- animal_df |> bind_rows(current_df)
}

gt(animal_df)
```

